Mike & Key ARC =E2=80=94 Fox Hunt Program

Open Planning & Build Resources

Collaborate on GitHub =E2=80=94 All Welcome

Hello fox hunters,

Our fox hunt planning, event documentation, and equipment build=20
resources are now available on GitHub for anyone to view and contribute to.


      What's in the Repository?

  * *Event planning documents* =E2=80=94 location scouting reports, hunt ar=
ea
    boundaries, park rules, and event logistics
  * *Build instructions* =E2=80=94 step-by-step guides for tape-measure Yag=
i
    antennas, attenuators, and other fox hunting equipment
  * *Email announcement templates* =E2=80=94 draft emails for upcoming even=
ts
    (like the ones sent to this list)
  * *Presentation materials* =E2=80=94 club meeting presentations and speak=
er notes
  * *Post-hunt reports* =E2=80=94 results, photos, and lessons learned from=
 past
    events


      How to Access

Visit the GitHub Repository <https://github.com/tsayles/ham-fox-hunting>

github.com/tsayles/ham-fox-hunting=20
<https://github.com/tsayles/ham-fox-hunting>

The repository is public =E2=80=94 no GitHub account required to browse. Al=
l=20
documentation is written in plain text Markdown, making it easy to read=20
directly on GitHub or download for offline reference.


      How to Contribute

Have ideas for improving hunt locations? Better build instructions?=20
Photos from a past hunt? Suggestions for new equipment? *We welcome=20
contributions!*

Here's how to participate:

  * *Submit a Pull Request* =E2=80=94 if you're familiar with Git/GitHub, f=
ork
    the repo, make your changes, and submit a PR. We'll review and merge
    useful contributions.
  * *Open an Issue* =E2=80=94 report problems, suggest improvements, or ask
    questions using GitHub Issues (no coding required).
  * *Email suggestions* =E2=80=94 not comfortable with GitHub? Send ideas,
    corrections, or files directly to TSayles@Soot-n-Smoke.com and I'll
    incorporate them.


      Why Open Source?

Open documentation means:

  * *Transparency* =E2=80=94 everyone can see hunt planning decisions and s=
afety
    considerations
  * *Collaboration* =E2=80=94 leverage the collective knowledge of our comm=
unity
  * *Reusability* =E2=80=94 other clubs can adapt our materials for their o=
wn
    fox hunt programs
  * *Version control* =E2=80=94 track changes over time and never lose
    historical documentation
  * *Permanence* =E2=80=94 the repository remains accessible even if indivi=
dual
    organizers change


      Getting Started with GitHub

New to GitHub? Here are some resources:

  * GitHub's Getting Started Guide <https://docs.github.com/en/get-started>
  * How to Submit a Pull Request <https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests>
  * Markdown Syntax Guide <https://www.markdownguide.org/basic-syntax/>

Or reach out =E2=80=94 I'm happy to help anyone get started with contributi=
ng,=20
whether you're a Git expert or have never used version control before.


      What's Next?

As we prepare for the August and September hunts, all planning documents=20
and event materials will be kept up-to-date in the repository. Watch the=20
repo (click "Watch" on GitHub) to get notifications when new content is=20
added.

Questions about the repository or how to contribute? Contact *Tom=20
Sayles, KE4HET* at TSayles@Soot-n-Smoke.com or reply to this message.

73,
Tom Sayles, KE4HET
Fox Hunt Program Coordinator

